#!/bin/bash
# kernel-update — print the current kernel update paths for this image.
#
# This used to tell users to rebuild a 6.17 sm8150-mainline tree on-device.
# That tree and the KERNEL_VERSION knob are gone; the shipped kernel is
# Kumar-Jy/linux nabu/7.3.y with the patch set in kernel/patches/.

cat <<'EOF'
Kernel updates on nabu-cachyos
==============================

1) Packaged kernel (once the [nabu-cachyos] repo is enabled, see
   packaging/README.md in the source repo):

     sudo pacman -Syu linux-nabu-cachyos
     nabu-kernel-flash stage            # rebuild + verify, writes nothing
     sudo nabu-kernel-flash flash --yes # back up boot_b, flash, verify

   The pacman hook only stages /boot/nabu-boot.img; flashing stays a
   separate, explicit step.

2) Host rebuild + fastboot (works today, no repo needed), on your build
   machine inside the nabu-cachyos checkout:

     bash kernel/build-kernel.sh        # or a full ./build.sh run
     # modules: copy output/kernel/modules/lib/modules/<release>/ into
     # /usr/lib/modules/ on the tablet (rsync/ssh), then from fastboot:
     fastboot flash boot_b output/boot.img
     fastboot reboot

There is NO supported on-device from-source kernel rebuild: the tablet
has no cross toolchain in the image, and a native build is exactly the
out-of-memory-prone path the host build exists to avoid.
EOF
